; DERIVED, not verbatim. Written by this library: the W3C Recommendation
; Securing Verifiable Credentials using JOSE and COSE
; (REC-vc-jose-cose-20250515) prints no CDDL. Source
; .cache/w3c-vc-jose-cose.bin, sha256
; 6c70cf8d7de9d8a3ff28a743fea9d569a67a402111c3245fdafbaa2ad6709af9; quotations
; of it are checked against .cache/w3c-vc-jose-cose.txt, sha256
; f02bd4966800863d533746c6c39ad267ada1b8bfcbb92186c204b617bb46c126. Quotations
; of other documents are checked against the copies README.md lists with their
; digests.
; Notation: CDDL, RFC 8610. Instance: the CBOR encoding of an
; application/vc+cose or application/vp+cose object, a COSE_Sign1 (RFC 9052
; section 4.2), tagged 18 or untagged (the source says neither; inferred).
;
; ROOTS. A CDDL tool validates against the first rule of a file, here vc-cose.
; To validate against another rule, put a line naming it first, for example
; root = vc-cose-should, in a copy of this file. Rules meant as roots:
;   vc-cose, vp-cose                 MUST level; one rule: no MUST tells them apart
;   vc-cose-should, vp-cose-should   adds the SHOULD and NOT RECOMMENDED statements
;   protected-must                   a decoded protected header map alone, MUST level
;   vc-protected-should, vp-protected-should   the same, SHOULD level
; A failure against a -should rule is a deviation from a recommendation,
; reported as a warning; a failure against a MUST rule is non-conformance.
; Each -should rule is at least as strict as its MUST rule.
;
; IMPORTED, re-typed from RFC 9052 (this file is derived, not a copy):
; "COSE_Sign1_Tagged = #6.18(COSE_Sign1)" (RFC 9052 section 4.2), its section
; 4.2 COSE_Sign1 = [Headers, payload : bstr / nil, signature : bstr]; section
; 3, Headers = (protected : empty_or_serialized_map, unprotected : header_map)
; and "empty_or_serialized_map = bstr .cbor header_map / bstr .size 0" (RFC
; 9052 section 3); section 3.1, Generic_Headers; section 1.5, "label = int /
; tstr" (RFC 9052 section 1.5) and values = any. Made binding by
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0047 and w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0050, and their presentation
; twins w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0053 and w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0056, which require
; COSE_Sign1 as specified in [RFC9052], and by section 3.3.3 paragraph 2,
; which carries no keyword: "The normative statements in Header Parameters,
; Claims, and CBOR Web Token (CWT) Claims in COSE Headers apply to securing
; credentials and presentations." (section 3.3.3) The three it names are RFC
; 9052 section 3.1, RFC 8392 section 3, and
; draft-ietf-cose-cwt-claims-in-headers-10 (the HTML links revision 10).
; From RFC 9052 section 3.1: 'When present, the "crit" header parameter MUST
; be placed in the protected-header-parameters bucket.' (RFC 9052 section 3.1)
; and "The array MUST have at least one value in it." (RFC 9052 section 3.1),
; so crit (2) has no unprotected entry; "This header parameter MUST be
; authenticated where the ability to do so exists." (RFC 9052 section 3.1),
; which the protected bucket or external data can do, so alg (1) is admitted
; in both buckets; and 'The "Initialization Vector" and "Partial
; Initialization Vector" header parameters MUST NOT both be present in the
; same security layer.' (RFC 9052 section 3.1), which holds within one map
; here, not across the two. From RFC 9596 section 2, which
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0048 cites by title for typ (16): "The syntax of this
; header parameter value is the same as the content type header parameter
; defined in Section 3.1 of [RFC9052];" (RFC 9596 section 2), so typ is uint /
; tstr, and 'The "typ" parameter MUST NOT be present in unprotected headers.'
; (RFC 9596 section 2) From the revision-10 draft: CWT-Claims = { *
; Claim-Label => any }, "Claim-Label = int / text"
; (draft-ietf-cose-cwt-claims-in-headers-10 section 2), and "The header
; parameter MUST only occur once in either the protected or unprotected header
; of a COSE structure." (draft-ietf-cose-cwt-claims-in-headers-10 section 2)
; Revision 10's table prints the label cell over two lines, "TBD (requested"
; (draft-ietf-cose-cwt-claims-in-headers-10 section 2) and "assignment 15)"
; (draft-ietf-cose-cwt-claims-in-headers-10 section 2); label 15 is RFC
; 9597's, its published form, whose row reads "| CWT | 15 | map |" (RFC 9597
; section 2), and Example 24's. From RFC 8392 sections 2, 3.1 and 4: claim
; keys 1 iss, 2 sub, 3 aud, 4 exp, 5 nbf, 6 iat, 7 cti with their value types;
; "The encoding is modified so that the leading tag 1 (epoch-based date/time)
; MUST be omitted." (RFC 8392 section 2) From RFC 8747 section 3, claim 8 cnf:
; 'The value of the "cnf" claim is a CBOR map' (RFC 8747 section 3).
;
; CUTS AND WILDCARDS. RFC 9052 writes Generic_Headers with =>, which carries
; no cut, beside the wildcard * label => values; by RFC 8610 section 3.5.4 a
; known label with a wrongly typed value then matches the wildcard, so {1:
; h'00'} matches RFC 9052's header_map as printed. Here every known label is
; an explicit entry written with a colon, and 'the ":" shortcut is actually
; defined to include the cut semantics.' (RFC 8610 section 3.5.4); and every
; wildcard is written over other-label or a narrower set, which excludes every
; known label of its map, so a wrongly typed known label can be matched by
; nothing even in a tool without the cut.
;
; WHAT THE SOURCE ADDS. w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0045 permits COSE, and
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0046 asks that the result be identified through content
; types. At the MUST level it adds nothing to the structure: its MUST
; statements defer to RFC 9052. w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0064 is the extension point
; the wildcards keep open. At the SHOULD level it adds the values of typ and
; content type: "(type) Header Parameter, SHOULD be application/vc+cose."
; (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0048); "The content type (3) header parameter SHOULD be
; application/vc." (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0049); their presentation twins
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0054 and w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0055; and
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0063. "CBOR Web Token (CWT) [RFC8392] Claims MAY be
; included in a COSE header parameter, as specified in
; I-D.ietf-cose-cwt-claims-in-headers." (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0061); CWT Claims
; (15) is the parameter it names.
;
; NOT HERE. w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0065 binds a kid when the key is expressed as a
; DID URL, and w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0072 and w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0074 require an
; absolute URL as kid under section 4.2's conditions; a COSE kid (4) is a
; bstr, so a URL kid is carried as bytes, which the source does not describe,
; and a CDDL .regexp applies to text only. Of kid, RFC 9052 says "For this
; reason, it can be placed in the unprotected-header-parameters bucket." (RFC
; 9052 section 3.1) The iss comparison of w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0066 and
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0067, and the proof-of-possession binding RECOMMENDED by
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0069, are cross-field-rules.derived.yaml X-01 to X-07.
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0062 cannot be checked: it leaves nothing in the object.
; RFC 9052's "Applications SHOULD verify that the same label does not occur in
; both the protected and unprotected header parameters." (RFC 9052 section 3)
; is a check across the two buckets and is not made.
;
; EXAMPLES. Every COSE_Sign1 the source prints (Examples 7, 8, 9, 18, 19, 20
; in hex, 23 in base64) is tagged 18, has the protected header {1: -35} alone,
; an empty unprotected map, a JSON-text payload and a 64-octet signature. -35
; is ES384, "| ES384 | -35 | SHA-384 | ECDSA w/ SHA-384 |" (RFC 9053 section
; 2.1), and RFC 9053 says "it is suggested that SHA-256 be used only with
; curve P-256, SHA-384 be used only with curve P-384, and SHA-512 be used only
; with curve P-521." (RFC 9053 section 2.1) and "The signature is encoded by
; converting the integers into byte strings of the same length as the key
; size." (RFC 9053 section 2.1), so with P-384 an ES384 signature is 96
; octets; 64 is what a 256-bit key gives. This grammar sees neither the
; algorithm's key nor the signature length; it matches all seven at the MUST
; level and fails all seven at the SHOULD level (no typ). Examples 24 to 26
; are CBOR diagnostic notation with elided bytes and cannot be encoded
; (README.md, Findings).

vc-cose = cose-sign1-must
vp-cose = cose-sign1-must

cose-sign1-must = #6.18(sign1-must) / sign1-must

; Revision 10 of the draft (imported, above): CWT Claims (15) occurs in one
; bucket or in neither, never in both, hence the two array shapes. Protected
; is a serialized header map or an empty bstr (RFC 9052 section 3); an empty
; one cannot hold the claims, so it belongs to the second shape.
sign1-must = sign1-claims-protected / sign1-claims-elsewhere
sign1-claims-protected = [
  protected: bstr .cbor protected-with-claims,
  unprotected: unprotected-no-claims,
  payload: bstr / nil,
  signature: bstr,
]
sign1-claims-elsewhere = [
  protected: bstr .cbor protected-no-claims / bstr .size 0,
  unprotected: unprotected-may-claims,
  payload: bstr / nil,
  signature: bstr,
]

; payload: bstr / nil, as RFC 9052 section 4.2. The bytes are the unsecured
; credential or presentation: "The unsecured verifiable credential is the
; unencoded COSE_Sign1 payload." (section 3.3.1); "The unsecured verifiable
; presentation is the unencoded COSE_Sign1 payload." (section 3.3.2). They are
; JSON text in every COSE example, not CBOR; this grammar does not look inside
; them, and payload.derived.schema.json applies to them decoded. nil is
; detached content, which RFC 9052 allows and the source neither shows nor
; forbids; Example 26 is titled with an attached payload.

; RFC 9052 section 3.1 Generic_Headers, with cuts (see CUTS AND WILDCARDS).
; content type is tstr / int as RFC 9052's CDDL fragment writes it; its Table
; 3 writes tstr / uint (README.md, Findings).
protected-common = (
  ? 1: int / tstr,          ; alg
  ? 2: [+ label],           ; crit, protected bucket only
  ? 3: tstr / int,          ; content type
  ? 4: bstr,                ; kid
  ? ( 5: bstr // 6: bstr ), ; IV, Partial IV
  ? 16: typ-value,          ; typ, protected bucket only (RFC 9596)
)
unprotected-common = (
  ? 1: int / tstr,          ; alg
  ? 3: tstr / int,          ; content type
  ? 4: bstr,                ; kid
  ? ( 5: bstr // 6: bstr ), ; IV, Partial IV
)

protected-must = protected-with-claims / protected-no-claims
protected-with-claims = {
  protected-common,
  15: cwt-claims-must,      ; CWT Claims
  * other-label => values,
}
protected-no-claims = {
  protected-common,
  * other-label => values,
}
; RFC 9596 section 2 and RFC 9052 section 3.1 (imported): typ (16) and crit
; (2) have no unprotected entry, and other-label excludes them, so either one
; in the unprotected map fails.
unprotected-may-claims = {
  unprotected-common,
  ? 15: cwt-claims-must,
  * other-label => values,
}
unprotected-no-claims = {
  unprotected-common,
  * other-label => values,
}

typ-value = uint / tstr     ; RFC 9596 section 2

; CWT-Claims of revision 10 with the RFC 8392 and RFC 8747 value types of the
; claims that have them, imported through w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0061, which says
; CWT claims are included as specified there, and through section 3.3.3
; paragraph 2 (above). aud follows RFC 8392 section 3.1.3, which admits an
; array; RFC 8392's own Table 1 lists aud as text string only.
cwt-claims-must = {
  ? 1: tstr,                ; iss
  ? 2: tstr,                ; sub
  ? 3: tstr / [* tstr],     ; aud, RFC 8392 section 3.1.3
  ? 4: int / float,         ; exp, NumericDate
  ? 5: int / float,         ; nbf
  ? 6: int / float,         ; iat
  ? 7: bstr,                ; cti
  ? 8: cnf-value,           ; cnf, RFC 8747
  * other-claim-label => any,
}
; cnf: RFC 8747 section 3 makes it a map; at least one member is inferred,
; since RFC 8747 section 3.1 requires the value to represent a single
; proof-of-possession key.
cnf-value = { + label => any }

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHOULD level
; typ (16) is required in the protected header, the only bucket RFC 9596
; allows it in. content type (3) stays optional, in either bucket, and is
; constrained only when present: w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0049 and
; w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0055 carry no When present condition, unlike their JOSE
; twins, but the sentence after each says "The content type (3) header
; parameter is optional" (section 3.3.1), and this level reads optional as
; optional (inferred; README.md, Findings). CWT Claims, when present, are
; protected-only at this level: "It is RECOMMENDED that the CWT Claims header
; parameter is used only in a protected header to avoid the contents being
; malleable." (draft-ietf-cose-cwt-claims-in-headers-10 section 2)
vc-cose-should = #6.18(vc-sign1-should) / vc-sign1-should
vp-cose-should = #6.18(vp-sign1-should) / vp-sign1-should

vc-sign1-should = [
  protected: bstr .cbor vc-protected-should,
  unprotected: vc-unprotected-should,
  payload: bstr / nil,
  signature: bstr,
]
vp-sign1-should = [
  protected: bstr .cbor vp-protected-should,
  unprotected: vp-unprotected-should,
  payload: bstr / nil,
  signature: bstr,
]

; "(type) Header Parameter, SHOULD be application/vc+cose."
; (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0048); "The content type (3) header parameter SHOULD be
; application/vc." (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0049). The presentation twins are
; "(type) Header Parameter, SHOULD be application/vp+cose."
; (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0054) and "The content type (3) header parameter SHOULD
; be application/vp." (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0055). The sentence after each
; content type SHOULD, which has no keyword, says "The content type (3) header
; parameter is optional, and can be used to express a more specific media type
; than application/vc when one is available." (section 3.3.1); so a more
; specific content type deviates here and may be justified. No example carries
; typ (16): every protected header in Examples 7, 8, 9, 18, 19, 20 and 23
; decodes to {1: -35} alone. Example 24 carries content type (3) but no typ.
vc-protected-should = {
  ? 1: int / tstr,
  ? 2: [+ label],
  ? 3: content-type-vc,
  ? 4: bstr,
  ? ( 5: bstr // 6: bstr ),
  ? 15: cwt-claims-should,
  16: typ-vc,
  * other-label => values,
}
vp-protected-should = {
  ? 1: int / tstr,
  ? 2: [+ label],
  ? 3: content-type-vp,
  ? 4: bstr,
  ? ( 5: bstr // 6: bstr ),
  ? 15: cwt-claims-should,
  16: typ-vp,
  * other-label => values,
}
; At this level label 15 has no unprotected entry (the draft's RECOMMENDED,
; above), and other-label excludes it.
vc-unprotected-should = {
  ? 1: int / tstr,
  ? 3: content-type-vc,
  ? 4: bstr,
  ? ( 5: bstr // 6: bstr ),
  * other-label => values,
}
vp-unprotected-should = {
  ? 1: int / tstr,
  ? 3: content-type-vp,
  ? 4: bstr,
  ? ( 5: bstr // 6: bstr ),
  * other-label => values,
}

; Media type names compare case-insensitively: "Both top-level type and
; subtype names are case-insensitive." (RFC 6838 section 4.2) (inferred to
; apply to typ and content type values, which name media types). A value with
; media type parameters does not match (the source names the bare type), and
; neither does a CoAP Content-Format number: the source registers none.
; .regexp is an XSD regular expression matched against the whole string.
typ-vc = tstr .regexp "[Aa][Pp][Pp][Ll][Ii][Cc][Aa][Tt][Ii][Oo][Nn]/[Vv][Cc][+][Cc][Oo][Ss][Ee]"
typ-vp = tstr .regexp "[Aa][Pp][Pp][Ll][Ii][Cc][Aa][Tt][Ii][Oo][Nn]/[Vv][Pp][+][Cc][Oo][Ss][Ee]"
content-type-vc = tstr .regexp "[Aa][Pp][Pp][Ll][Ii][Cc][Aa][Tt][Ii][Oo][Nn]/[Vv][Cc]"
content-type-vp = tstr .regexp "[Aa][Pp][Pp][Ll][Ii][Cc][Aa][Tt][Ii][Oo][Nn]/[Vv][Pp]"

; "Use of the nbf (Not Before) claim is NOT RECOMMENDED, as it makes little
; sense to attempt to assign a future date to a signature."
; (w3c-vc-jose-cose#R-0063) Label 5, nbf (RFC 8392 section 3.1.5), has no
; entry at this level and other-claim-label excludes it.
cwt-claims-should = {
  ? 1: tstr,
  ? 2: tstr,
  ? 3: tstr / [* tstr],
  ? 4: int / float,
  ? 6: int / float,
  ? 7: bstr,
  ? 8: cnf-value,
  * other-claim-label => any,
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; labels
; other-label excludes every header label given an explicit entry anywhere in
; this file (1 to 6, 15, 16); other-claim-label excludes claim keys 1 to 8.
; Ranges are inclusive (RFC 8610 section 2.2.2.1).
label = int / tstr
values = any
other-label = (int .lt 1) / 7..14 / (int .gt 16) / tstr
other-claim-label = (int .lt 1) / (int .gt 8) / tstr
